title: primeval_context
---
// The primeval trail's people (the jungle dimension): the ranger at the
// trailhead by the gate, the paleontologist at the tar pits past the end of
// the trail. One node per resident, named `primeval_<resident>`.
//
// Everyday talk only (Jon, 2026-10-29: "we are world building not quest
// building"): no flags, no errands.
//
// Never played. No placement names this node; it declares the zone.
===

title: primeval_brandvold
---
Ranger Brandvold: Welcome to the primeval trail. Stay on the path, carry water, and do not make eye contact with the chickens.
-> The chickens?
    Ranger Brandvold: Everybody worries about the raptors. The raptors are honest. The chickens know what they used to be.
-> What's at the end of the trail?
    Ranger Brandvold: The canopy, then the tar pits, then a paleontologist who will not leave. Past him, nobody has filled in a form.
    Ranger Brandvold: There is also a temple with a king in it. I do not patrol the temple. The temple patrols me.
-> Is the trail safe?
    Ranger Brandvold: It is marked. That is not the same thing, and the sign says so in small print.
-> Thanks, ranger.
    Ranger Brandvold: Sign back in when you return. If you return, I mean. Most do. The form is optimistic.
===

title: primeval_feld
---
Dr Feld: Don't step there. Or there. Fine, step there, but gently. That is a layer.
-> What are you digging for?
    Dr Feld: Fossils. The tar keeps everything: mammoths, sabre-tooths, a lunchbox I think was mine.
    Dr Feld: The difficulty is that most of my specimens are still alive and running past the tent.
-> Isn't it dangerous?
    Dr Feld: For the fieldwork, yes. For the field, more so. A triceratops sat on my survey grid on Tuesday.
    Dr Feld: I have recorded it as a sedimentary event.
-> What's the mammoth doing here?
    Dr Feld: Sinking, very slowly, for about thirty thousand years. People walk along its back. I have stopped asking them not to.
-> Good luck with the dig.
    Dr Feld: Luck is for gamblers. I have a brush and a great deal of time.
===

title: primeval_tock
---
Professor Tock: Shh. Not you, the sky. I'm listening for it.
-> Listening for what?
    Professor Tock: The meteor. The big one. Everything down there has been waiting for it for sixty-six million years, they just don't know it.
    Professor Tock: Somebody ought to be watching when it comes. I have a chair, a telescope and a thermos. That is the whole department.
-> Isn't the volcano dangerous?
    Professor Tock: Compared to the meteor? It's a candle. I warm my hands on it.
-> What if it never comes?
    Professor Tock: Then I'll have had a very long sit with a very good view. There are worse careers. Paleontology, for one. Ask Feld.
-> Enjoy the view.
    Professor Tock: Wave if you see anything on fire coming down. Not the lava. The lava's always on fire.
===

title: primeval_twitchell
---
Mildred Twitchell: Shh. Shh! Get down. You'll put up the swallows.
-> What are you watching?
    Mildred Twitchell: The rookery. Swallows, a shoebill, and penguins. Penguins! In a jungle, beside a volcano, a long way from any ice.
    Mildred Twitchell: I stopped asking why in the first week. Now I just count them. There are far too many.
-> Are the swallows African or European?
    Mildred Twitchell: African. Unladen. I've timed them. Please don't ask me how fast; people always do, and then they laugh.
-> What is the shoebill doing?
    Mildred Twitchell: Standing still. It has been standing still since Tuesday. It is the most frightening bird I have ever seen, and I have seen a cassowary angry.
-> I'll leave you to it.
    Mildred Twitchell: Quietly, please. And if a penguin follows you, it isn't yours. Don't let it tell you it is.
===

title: primeval_dlamini
---
Ranger Dlamini: (into the radio) Station to Ridge, the walker's left the station. Over. (to you) Sorry. Welcome to the ranger station. Don't touch the jars.
-> What do you do here?
    Ranger Dlamini: I keep the radio. Brandvold walks the trail, Feld digs, Tock watches the sky, Twitchell counts the penguins. Every one of them checks in. I know where every walker on the trail is.
    Ranger Dlamini: And what is eating them. That's the useful part.
-> What is in the jars?
    Ranger Dlamini: Ferns, eggs, and compys we've rescued from tar. They are very small and very annoyed and they bite through the lids. I've stopped labelling them. They know who they are.
-> What is in the lost-property box?
    Ranger Dlamini: One boot, a monocle, an umbrella. All from the same walker. We never found the rest of him. He sent a postcard from the mountain, so I think he's fine.
-> I'll be on my way.
    Ranger Dlamini: Check in at the ridge. If you don't, I'll assume the worst, and send Brandvold, and he'll assume the worst louder.
===
